From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 23:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] DVDInputStreamBluray: read a clip ahead, so a stalled source buffers

A Blu-ray played from a folder or an image through Kodi's filesystem has
libbluray open its clips with plain CFile reads, on the player thread and
with no cache. When the share stops answering, the player thread blocks in
one read for as long as the outage lasts. Nothing that would buffer can
run: the video player declares a still frame, the clock runs on, and when
the share answers again playback jumps ahead by the length of the outage.
A share that resets the connection instead fails the read, and libbluray
takes a failed read for a damaged unit: it skips it, and after enough of
them the title ends.

A clip (.m2ts) is now opened as any video file is, with READ_AUDIO_VIDEO,
so it is read ahead into a file cache as the cache settings say, and the
input stream reports that cache's status to the player. The player then
sees the source run dry and buffers, as it does for other files. The
other files of the disc are opened as before.

The player measures the cache in seconds and sets its throttle from the
length of the stream, which it took from the demuxer alone. A Blu-ray's
demuxer reads a stream of unknown size and reports no length, so the
cache read as -1 s and was never throttled; the input stream's own
length is used where the demuxer has none.

The cache is throttled to the title's bitrate, as CDVDInputStreamFile's
is: without it every clip cache read ahead at its 1 MiB/s default, below
a second of video at Blu-ray rates. A clip opened at a join starts empty
while the one before it still plays from the player's queues, so it
reports nothing until it has held a second of video once. libbluray
sizes a clip by seeking to its end and back; on a cache that would drop
it and wait on the source twice per clip, so the seek to the end is only
noted and answered with the length.

Once the rest of a play item is cached, its end is no longer a stall,
so a share lost in a play item's last stretch would only show when
libbluray opened the next one, through an uncached open and an empty
cache. The next play item's file is therefore opened and read ahead
then, from where that play item starts: play items need not start at the
start of their file, and a disc that branches between scenes plays many
out of one file. Where each starts and ends is taken from the entry
points of its clip, from the copy of the .clpi that libbluray read when
it opened the title, which costs no disc access. libbluray closes the
file it reads at every join and opens the next play item's again; it is
handed the file opened ahead, or the cache it read the last play item
from if that already holds the next. One it does not ask for is dropped.

That open runs on a thread of its own: a short play item is cached to
its end as soon as it is reached, and the share can have stopped
answering by then. Until it is done the play item being played reports
only what is left of it, and no end of input, so the player buffers
before the join rather than block in libbluray there; a job is given a
second first. A stalled open does not fail: one that fails five times is
a file that cannot be opened on a share that answers, and the join opens
it as it did before. A job left running when the disc closes or the
playlist changes ends on its own.

A read that still meets a stall, with nothing left in the cache, is
retried until the source answers or the player stops, a second at a time
as the cache waits. libbluray never sees it. Directory listings and the
library scan read as before and fail fast.

The read ahead is a class of its own, CBlurayClipReader: the input
stream hands it libbluray's clip opens and the play item being read, at
whose event it takes the playlist's play items from libbluray.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
